# A LITERAL BRACE, IN THE FACE THAT ALREADY HAS ONE.
#
# `law.literal.bytes` binds "single quotes are the one canonical byte sequence
# literal face". Measured, that face does NOT interpolate: a `{` inside it is a
# byte, not a hole opener, and no STR-1 diagnostic is raised. This file is the
# oracle for that fact, because it is the only spelling of a literal brace that
# `docs/text-law.md` finds already admitted, and the migration out of the 419
# hole-free brace sites rests on it staying true.
#
# WHAT WOULD BREAK THIS FILE. Teaching the byte face to interpolate — the
# obvious "make the two literal faces consistent" change — turns every line
# below into a different answer. That is the change this oracle exists to catch.
#
# THE FACE'S TWO MEASURED LIMITS are recorded here rather than in prose, because
# a limit nobody can run is a limit that gets forgotten:
#
#   * it does not decode escapes — `'a\nb'` is FOUR bytes, not three, so no
#     literal needing both `\n` and `{` can move here;
#   * it cannot spell its own delimiter — `'\''` prints a BACKSLASH and a quote.
#
# Together those are why `docs/text-law.md` rules that this face closes 419 of
# the 547 sites and cannot close the remaining 128.
#
# WHAT THIS FILE DELIBERATELY DOES NOT COVER: `[[ ... ]]`. It is also raw
# (measured), but `law.literal.text` binds "lua long brackets are not canonical
# text faces" and C0 §42 files it under `audit`, so pinning it here would make a
# retirement candidate load-bearing. Its measurement lives in `docs/text-law.md`
# with the command that produced it.
# expect: {"k": 1, "n": {"d": 2}} awk {print $1} lone { brace 23 4 prefix-{
# @corpus current

json: str = '{"k": 1, "n": {"d": 2}}'
prog: str = 'awk {print $1}'
lone: str = 'lone { brace'

print(json)
print(prog)
print(lone)

# The byte face is descriptor-compatible with `str`: it answers `:len()` and it
# concatenates with a text literal. Without this the face would be a dead end
# rather than a migration target.
print("{json:len()}")

# The escape limit, as a running number rather than a claim. The text face
# answers 3 for the same four source characters.
esc = 'a\nb'
print("{esc:len()}")

print("prefix-" .. '{')
